@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `usca-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `usca-holidays`
IMPORT `usca-general-provisions`
IMPORT `usca-operation-of-statutes`
IMPORT `usca-further-asks`

-- Tests of usca-further-asks.l4.  Each expected value is decided from the section cited above the assertion, before the first run.  A failing assertion is a finding.
-- A REFUSED assertion gives the message (BECAUSE) so that it shows which REFUSE fired.  The weekdays of the dates named were taken from a calendar, not from the module.

GIVEN d IS A DATE
      n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Moment
`a moment` d n MEANS
    Moment WITH `the day` IS d, `seconds after midnight` IS n

-- A record in which every list has a distinct member, to show which fields the Section 135 wrappers copy.
GIVETH A `Days supplied by the caller`
`a record with every list filled` MEANS
    `Days supplied by the caller` WITH
        `Lunar New Year days` IS LIST (YMD 2026 2 17)
        `Diwali days` IS LIST (YMD 2026 11 7)
        `days appointed by the President or Governor for a public fast, thanksgiving or holiday` IS LIST (YMD 2026 5 4)
        `Thursdays appointed as Thanksgiving Day` IS LIST (YMD 2026 11 26)
        `Good Friday days` IS LIST (YMD 2026 4 3)
        `days on which March 31 is observed instead` IS LIST (YMD 2026 3 27)
        `March 31 itself stays a holiday in a year in which it is observed on another day` IS FALSE
        `judicial holidays specified in Code Civ. Proc. section 135` IS LIST (YMD 2026 1 2)
        `days a public office is closed for the whole day` IS LIST (YMD 2026 6 3)
        `bank closings under the Bank Extraordinary Situation Closing Act` IS LIST (YMD 2026 7 6)

§ `Gov. Code § 6701(c): the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31`

-- 31 March 2026 is a Tuesday: the preceding Friday is 27 March, the preceding Monday 30 March, the following Friday 3 April.
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2026 EQUALS (LIST (YMD 2026 3 27), (YMD 2026 3 30), (YMD 2026 4 3))
-- 31 March 2027 is a Wednesday: 26 March, 29 March, 2 April.
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2027 EQUALS (LIST (YMD 2027 3 26), (YMD 2027 3 29), (YMD 2027 4 2))
-- 31 March 2022 is a Thursday: 25 March, 28 March, 1 April.
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2022 EQUALS (LIST (YMD 2022 3 25), (YMD 2022 3 28), (YMD 2022 4 1))
-- Monday (2025), Friday (2023, 2028), Saturday (2029) and Sunday (2024, 2030): the section speaks only of a Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday.
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2025 EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2023 EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2028 EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2029 EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2024 EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` 2030 EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2026 3 27)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2026 3 30)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2026 4 3)
-- The following Monday is not among the three days.
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2026 4 6))
-- March 31 itself is not one of the other days.
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2026 3 31))
-- 31 March 2025 is a Monday: no other day under (c).
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2025 3 28))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` (YMD 2025 4 4))

§ `Gov. Code § 6701(d): a county's alternate day for a holiday that falls on a Saturday`

-- 4 July 2026 is a Saturday and a holiday: a board of supervisors may provide an alternate day.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county board of supervisors` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 7 4)
-- 25 December 2027 is a Saturday.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county board of supervisors` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2027 12 25)
-- 4 July 2025 is a Friday, not a Saturday.
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county board of supervisors` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2025 7 4))
-- A Saturday that is not a holiday (11 July 2026).
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county board of supervisors` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 7 11))
-- The power is the board's; a city council has none under (d).
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `the legislative body of a city` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 7 4))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `the legislative body of a district` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 7 4))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county agency` `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 7 4))
-- A holiday the caller supplies counts, since the state calendar includes it: Diwali on a Saturday (7 November 2026) in the record; another Saturday (6 June 2026) does not.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county board of supervisors` (`a record with every list filled`) (YMD 2026 11 7)
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` `a county board of supervisors` (`a record with every list filled`) (YMD 2026 6 6))

§ `Gov. Code § 6703: who must be closed on which day`

-- (a) Admission Day, 9 September 2026 (a Wednesday): state offices, state institutions and the University of California.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state office or agency` (YMD 2026 9 9)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state institution` (YMD 2026 9 9)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the University of California` (YMD 2026 9 9)
-- (b) Veterans Day, 11 November 2026 (a Wednesday): state offices, state institutions and the Legislature; the University of California only by intent.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state office or agency` (YMD 2026 11 11)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state institution` (YMD 2026 11 11)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the Legislature` (YMD 2026 11 11)
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the University of California` (YMD 2026 11 11))
-- 11 November 2023 is a Saturday: Friday 10 November.  11 November 2029 is a Sunday: Monday 12 November.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state office or agency` (YMD 2023 11 10)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the Legislature` (YMD 2023 11 10)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state office or agency` (YMD 2029 11 12)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the Legislature` (YMD 2029 11 12)
-- A day that is none of those.
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a state office or agency` (YMD 2026 6 1))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the Legislature` (YMD 2026 6 1))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the University of California` (YMD 2026 6 1))
-- The Legislature on Admission Day: (a) does not name it (F-6703leg).
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the Legislature` (YMD 2026 9 9) BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 6703(a) names the public offices of the state, state institutions and the University of California for Admission Day and does not say whether the Legislature is among them; § 6703(b) names the Legislature for Veterans Day only"
-- A county, a city and a district are not named.
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a county board of supervisors` (YMD 2026 9 9))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `a county agency` (YMD 2026 11 11))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the legislative body of a city` (YMD 2026 11 11))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` `the legislative body of a district` (YMD 2026 9 9))

§ `Gov. Code § 6704: Saturdays as holidays for the offices of a city or district`

#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6704 gives the body the power to make every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` `the legislative body of a city`
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6704 gives the body the power to make every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` `the legislative body of a district`
-- A county is not named (F-6704county).
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6704 gives the body the power to make every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` `a county board of supervisors`)
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6704 gives the body the power to make every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` `a state office or agency`)
-- "provision shall be made for the continuance of essential public services such as police and fire protection".
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6704 requires provision for the continuance of essential public services such as police and fire protection` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6704 requires provision for the continuance of essential public services such as police and fire protection` FALSE)

§ `Gov. Code § 6705: a special or limited holiday`

#ASSERT `a holiday is a special or limited holiday under Gov. Code § 6705` TRUE FALSE
-- Appointed to be generally observed by all classes: not special or limited.
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday is a special or limited holiday under Gov. Code § 6705` FALSE TRUE)
-- Applying to a special class and also appointed for all: the section's definition says "and not appointed to be generally observed".
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday is a special or limited holiday under Gov. Code § 6705` TRUE TRUE)
-- Applying to no special class and not generally observed either.
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday is a special or limited holiday under Gov. Code § 6705` FALSE FALSE)
-- A party outside the class the proclamation limits performs as usual; the limited class does not.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6705 has a contract performed and business transacted as usual by the party on a special or limited holiday` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6705 has a contract performed and business transacted as usual by the party on a special or limited holiday` TRUE)

§ `Gov. Code § 6706 and § 6707`

#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6706 applies to an act appointed to be performed on a particular day by` `a law`
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6706 applies to an act appointed to be performed on a particular day by` `a contract`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 6706 applies to an act appointed to be performed on a particular day by` `a court order` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 6706 speaks of a day appointed by law or contract; it does not say whether a day fixed by a court order is one"
-- "may be performed", not "shall".
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6706 requires the act to be performed on the next business day`)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6707 gives the next business day for a filing with` `a state office or agency`
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6707 gives the next business day for a filing with` `a county agency`)
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6707 gives the next business day for a filing with` `the legislative body of a city`)

§ `Gov. Code § 6801, § 6802, § 6804, § 6808(b)`

#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6801 computes time according to the Gregorian calendar`
-- 2100 and 2200 are common years; 2400 and 2000 are leap years; every fourth year is a leap year.
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 2100 EQUALS 365
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 2200 EQUALS 365
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 2400 EQUALS 366
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 2000 EQUALS 366
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 2024 EQUALS 366
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 2025 EQUALS 365
-- § 6802 names 1900 as a common year; a hundredth year before 1900 is not named (F-6802).
#ASSERT `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 1900 EQUALS 365
#ASSERT REFUSED `days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` 1800 BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 6802 names 1900 and later hundredth years, and says every fourth year is a leap year by usage; for an earlier hundredth year it does not say whether the Gregorian exception of § 6801 applies"
-- "unless otherwise expressed".
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 6804 gives the word month the meaning of a calendar month` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 6804 gives the word month the meaning of a calendar month` TRUE)
-- 2 a.m.
#ASSERT `the clock reading, in seconds after midnight, at which daylight saving time begins and ends under Gov. Code § 6808(b)` EQUALS 7200
-- The clock readings between 1 a.m. and 2 a.m. on the November day occur twice and are refused (F-dst); 2 a.m. standard is a reading of its own.
#ASSERT REFUSED `daylight saving time at a clock reading on the day it ends` 5400 BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 6808(b): daylight saving time ends at 2 a.m., so the clock reads between 1 a.m. and 2 a.m. twice that day; the text does not say which of the two a bare reading means, nor whether 2 a.m. there is read on the daylight clock or the standard one"

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(b), § 12b, § 13a, § 13b, § 33`

#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `Section 659`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `Section 659a`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `Section 921`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `another provision of the Code of Civil Procedure`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `a provision of another code or statute`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `an ordinance`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `a rule`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `a regulation`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` `a contract` BECAUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(b) applies to provisions of law in a code, statute, ordinance, rule or regulation; it does not say whether a period fixed by a contract is one"
-- § 12b: a city, county, state or public office, other than a branch office, closed for the whole of the day.
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `a city office` TRUE
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `a county office` TRUE
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `a state office` TRUE
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `another public office` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `a city office` FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `a branch office` TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` `a private office` TRUE)
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b applies to the computation of time under` `Code Civ. Proc. § 12`
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b applies to the computation of time under` `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Code Civ. Proc. § 12b applies to the computation of time under` `Gov. Code § 6800` BECAUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12b names Sections 12 and 12a of the Code of Civil Procedure; it does not say whether a day it makes a holiday counts under Gov. Code § 6800, which says the same as § 12"
-- § 13a and § 13b give a permission, never a duty.
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 13a or § 13b requires an act to be performed on` `a special holiday`)
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 13a or § 13b requires an act to be performed on` `a Saturday`)
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 13a or § 13b requires an act to be performed on` `a Sunday or another holiday that is not a special holiday`)
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 33 requires a prosecuting attorney to assist in the civil resolution of a violation`)

§ `Gov. Code § 14, § 12.5, § 24, § 16.5(a)(5)`

#ASSERT `the force of the word as written in Gov. Code § 14` "shall" EQUALS mandatory
#ASSERT `the force of the word as written in Gov. Code § 14` "may" EQUALS permissive
#ASSERT REFUSED `the force of the word as written in Gov. Code § 14` "must" BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 14 says that shall is mandatory and may is permissive; it does not say what any other word, such as must, is"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the force of the word as written in Gov. Code § 14` "will" BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 14 says that shall is mandatory and may is permissive; it does not say what any other word, such as must, is"
#ASSERT NOT (`the declaration of Gov. Code § 12.5 or § 24 is amendatory of existing law`)
#ASSERT `the latest date for the Secretary of State's initial regulations under Gov. Code § 16.5(a)(5)` EQUALS YMD 1997 1 1
#ASSERT `the fewest public hearings the Secretary of State must hold before adopting the regulations under Gov. Code § 16.5(a)(5)` EQUALS 1

§ `Gov. Code § 9602, § 9604, § 9605(b), § 9607(b), § 9608, § 9610, § 9611`

-- A resolution takes effect on filing: in effect at the moment of filing and after, not before, and not at all while unfiled.
#ASSERT `a concurrent or joint resolution is in effect at the moment` (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 1) 36000) `given that it was filed with the Secretary of State at` (JUST (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 1) 36000))
#ASSERT `a concurrent or joint resolution is in effect at the moment` (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 2) 0) `given that it was filed with the Secretary of State at` (JUST (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 1) 36000))
#ASSERT NOT (`a concurrent or joint resolution is in effect at the moment` (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 1) 35999) `given that it was filed with the Secretary of State at` (JUST (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 1) 36000)))
#ASSERT NOT (`a concurrent or joint resolution is in effect at the moment` (`a moment` (YMD 2026 5 1) 36000) `given that it was filed with the Secretary of State at` NOTHING)
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9604 applies to a reference made by` `a statute of the State`
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9604 applies to a reference made by` `a charter`
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9604 applies to a reference made by` `an ordinance`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 9604 applies to a reference made by` `a contract` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9604 speaks of a reference made by a statute, charter or ordinance; it does not say whether a reference in a contract is one"
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 9604 applies to a reference made by` `a regulation` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9604 speaks of a reference made by a statute, charter or ordinance; it does not say whether a reference in a regulation is one"
-- (1) "conclusively presumed", (2) "presumed".
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9605(b) makes the presumption conclusive` `the presumption of paragraph (1)`
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 9605(b) makes the presumption conclusive` `the presumption of paragraph (2)`)
-- § 9607(b): only a later statute that deletes or extends the date, chaptered before it.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9607(b) revives the terminated or repealed law` TRUE (YMD 2026 6 1) (YMD 2026 12 31) (YMD 2027 1 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 9607(b) revives the terminated or repealed law` TRUE (YMD 2026 12 31) (YMD 2026 12 31) (YMD 2027 1 1))
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 9607(b) revives the terminated or repealed law` FALSE (YMD 2026 6 1) (YMD 2026 12 31) (YMD 2027 1 1))
-- § 9608: a law creating a criminal offense; not one creating civil liability.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9608 leaves an act already committed open to proceedings and punishment after the law ends` `a law creating a criminal offense` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 9608 leaves an act already committed open to proceedings and punishment after the law ends` `a law creating a criminal offense` TRUE)
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 9608 leaves an act already committed open to proceedings and punishment after the law ends` `a law creating civil liability` FALSE BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9608 speaks of a law creating a criminal offense; it does not say what follows when a law creating civil liability ends"
-- § 9610: a State officer or employee; a county or a city officer is not named.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9610 makes the salary fixed by statute payable only if moneys are made available by another provision of law` `a State officer or employee`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 9610 makes the salary fixed by statute payable only if moneys are made available by another provision of law` `a county officer or employee` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9610 speaks of the salary of a State officer or employee; it does not say what holds for a county officer or employee"
#ASSERT REFUSED `Gov. Code § 9610 makes the salary fixed by statute payable only if moneys are made available by another provision of law` `a city officer or employee` BECAUSE "Gov. Code § 9610 speaks of the salary of a State officer or employee; it does not say what holds for a city officer or employee"
-- § 9611: a temporary suspension, or a modification declared effective for a limited period; a permanent one is neither.
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9611 keeps the original provision from being deemed repealed` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Gov. Code § 9611 keeps the original provision from being deemed repealed` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Gov. Code § 9611 keeps the original provision from being deemed repealed` FALSE FALSE)

§ `Civ. Code § 14(b), § 17, § 20, § 22.2, § 23.6; Code Civ. Proc. § 9`

#ASSERT `Civ. Code § 14(b) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) give the word the signification they state` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Civ. Code § 14(b) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) give the word the signification they state` TRUE)
#ASSERT `a signification stated in Civ. Code § 14(b) or Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) is displaced` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`a signification stated in Civ. Code § 14(b) or Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) is displaced` FALSE)
#ASSERT `a requirement of the Civil Code that a notice be mailed by certified mail is met by` `certified mail`
#ASSERT REFUSED `a requirement of the Civil Code that a notice be mailed by certified mail is met by` `registered mail` BECAUSE "Civ. Code § 17 says that certified mail is a sufficient compliance where registered mail is required; it does not say that registered mail is sufficient where certified mail is required"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a requirement of the Civil Code that a notice be mailed by certified mail is met by` `another method of mailing` BECAUSE "Civ. Code § 17 says that certified mail is a sufficient compliance where registered mail is required; it says nothing of a requirement of certified mail"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the repeal by the Civil Code affects a private statute` BECAUSE "Civ. Code § 20 says nothing of a private statute; Code Civ. Proc. § 18 does, and Civ. Code § 20 does not say that it is to be read the same way"
#ASSERT `Civ. Code § 22.2 makes the law of England the rule of decision in the courts of this State` `the common law of England` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Civ. Code § 22.2 makes the law of England the rule of decision in the courts of this State` `the common law of England` TRUE)
#ASSERT REFUSED `Civ. Code § 22.2 makes the law of England the rule of decision in the courts of this State` `a statute of England` FALSE BECAUSE "Civ. Code § 22.2 speaks of the common law of England; it does not say whether an English statute is the rule of decision"
-- § 23.6 speaks of sections of the same chapter or article.
#ASSERT `Civ. Code § 23.6 makes the section last in numerical order prevail` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Civ. Code § 23.6 makes the section last in numerical order prevail` FALSE)
-- § 9 needs a limitation that has begun to run and a limitation the code prescribes.
#ASSERT `Code Civ. Proc. § 9 deems the time already run part of the time the code prescribes` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 9 deems the time already run part of the time the code prescribes` TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Code Civ. Proc. § 9 deems the time already run part of the time the code prescribes` FALSE TRUE)
-- "No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared": the Civil Code and the Code of Civil Procedure say it; the deposited Government Code does not.
#ASSERT `the code says that no part of it is retroactive unless expressly so declared` `the Civil Code`
#ASSERT `the code says that no part of it is retroactive unless expressly so declared` `the Code of Civil Procedure`
#ASSERT REFUSED `the code says that no part of it is retroactive unless expressly so declared` `the Government Code` BECAUSE "the deposited sections of the Government Code have no sentence saying that no part of it is retroactive unless expressly so declared; Gov. Code § 4 speaks of actions begun and rights accrued before the code took effect"

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 10, § 12, § 12a: the Section 135 days, stated or left out`

-- The caller has not said what Section 135 specifies: the four functions refuse.
#ASSERT REFUSED `a holiday under Code Civ. Proc. § 10, the Section 135 days being` NOTHING `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25) BECAUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 10 makes the judicial holidays specified in Section 135 holidays, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a holiday for computing time under Code Civ. Proc. § 12, the Section 135 days being` NOTHING `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25) BECAUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12 counts the holidays of Code Civ. Proc. § 10, which include the judicial holidays specified in Section 135, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), read literally, the Section 135 days being` NOTHING `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25) BECAUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) makes the holidays specified in Section 135 holidays for the extension, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
#ASSERT REFUSED `a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), taking in the Sundays of § 10, the Section 135 days being` NOTHING `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25) BECAUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) makes the holidays specified in Section 135 holidays for the extension, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
-- The caller says there are none: 25 December 2026 (a Friday) is then not a holiday under any of them.
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday under Code Civ. Proc. § 10, the Section 135 days being` (JUST EMPTY) `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25))
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday for computing time under Code Civ. Proc. § 12, the Section 135 days being` (JUST EMPTY) `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25))
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), read literally, the Section 135 days being` (JUST EMPTY) `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25))
#ASSERT NOT (`a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), taking in the Sundays of § 10, the Section 135 days being` (JUST EMPTY) `no day is supplied` (YMD 2026 12 25))
